# Caddy in front of the Elymum game server.
# - Automatic HTTPS: Caddy gets and renews a Let's Encrypt certificate for GAME_DOMAIN by itself
#   (the DNS A record must point at this server, and ports 80 and 443 must be open).
# - WebSockets (/ws) are proxied as is; reverse_proxy handles the upgrade.
# - Client IP: reverse_proxy sets X-Forwarded-For (and -Proto, -Host) itself. With no `trusted_proxies`
#   configured (none here, on purpose) Caddy ignores any X-Forwarded-For a client sends and passes exactly
#   one hop, the real client address. The app reads it with TRUST_PROXY=1 / TRUST_PROXY_HOPS=1 (set in
#   docker-compose.yml), so per-IP limits see each player's own address. Do not add `trusted_proxies` here.
#   Behind Cloudflare use Caddyfile.cloudflare instead (deploy/vps/README.md, "Cloudflare"): it trusts only
#   Cloudflare's ranges and hands the app the same single hop.
# - Slow and oversized requests (slowloris, header and body floods) are cut here, before they reach the app;
#   the app has its own limits too (15 s for headers, 30 s per request, 64 KiB bodies, per-IP rate limits).
#   A WebSocket is not affected by these timeouts once it is upgraded.
# Reload after an edit: deploy/vps/compose.sh exec caddy caddy reload --config /etc/caddy/Caddyfile

{
	servers {
		timeouts {
			read_header 10s
			read_body 30s
			idle 2m
		}
		max_header_size 16KB
	}
}

{$GAME_DOMAIN} {
	encode zstd gzip

	# the largest real body is a 64 KiB profile picture
	request_body {
		max_size 1MB
	}

	header {
		Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=31536000"
		X-Content-Type-Options nosniff
		-Server
	}

	reverse_proxy app:8787 {
		# stream responses (the admin backup download) instead of buffering them
		flush_interval -1
	}
}
